INT. NEONATAL ICU - NURSES' STATION - 3 A.M.

Soft monitors. A mug of cold tea. Six people around a whiteboard.

DR. ELOISE FAURE (50s, attending) caps a marker. NURSE DESMOND ACHEBE (30s) sips his coffee. NURSE LUPE TALAKAI (40s) scrolls a chart. DR. ANIKA VERMA (30s, resident) hugs a clipboard. RESPIRATORY THERAPIST BO LINDGREN (20s) leans on the counter. CHAPLAIN MIRIAM GOLD (60s) sits quietly in the corner.

FAURE
Okay. Bed six stabilised, bed nine's weaning, bed eleven is the one I'm watching.

ACHEBE
Eleven's sats have been drifting.

TALAKAI
Ninety-one, ninety, ninety-one.

VERMA
That's within range.

FAURE
It's within range for a healthy infant. Not for this one.

LINDGREN
I can bump the flow.

FAURE
Do it. Slowly.

GOLD
The parents asked if they could come in.

FAURE
At four. If nothing changes.

GOLD
They're afraid it will.

FAURE
So am I.

A pause. The monitors keep their quiet rhythm.

ACHEBE
Everything looks calm.

TALAKAI
That's what scares me.

Behind them, a single alarm begins to sound. Soft, then not. Bed eleven.
